science/article/pii/S2667325826004255)\n  57. NASA probe brings back samples from asteroid Bennu, discovers diverse amino acids: International research group confirmed the discovery of \"Materials responsible for life\" (https://sj.jst.go.jp/news/202503/n0324-01k.html)\n  58. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/cg901303a)\n  59. Thermal decomposition of the amino acids glycine, cysteine, aspartic acid, asparagine, glutamic acid, glutamine, arginine and histidine - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5807855/)\n  60. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231233025_Phase_Transformations_of_Glutamic_Acid_and_Its_Decomposition_Products)\n  61. The effects of temperature, pH and redox state on the stability of glutamic acid in hydrothermal fluids - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016703714001537)\n  62. Enantiomeric Excesses of Acid Labile Amino Acid Precursors of the Murchison Meteorite - NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS) (https://ntrs.nasa.gov/search.jsp?R=19990021044&hterms=pyroglutamic+acid&qs=N%3D0%26Ntk%3DAll%26Ntx%3Dmode%2Bmatchall%26Ntt%3Dpyroglutamic%2Bacid)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This synthesis network integrates both Strecker-nitrile and \u03b1-ketoglutarate (\u03b1-KG) metabolic-analog routes, reflecting the dual families of prebiotic glutamate synthesis identified in the literature. It explicitly includes C4 precursor bottlenecks (3-cyanopropanal and \u03b1-KG), nitrene/nitrile channels (via cyanosulfidic photochemistry and atmospheric discharge/Spark-Strecker), protometabolic Fe-catalyzed routes, Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin chemistry, and hydrothermal thermodynamic routes. Parallel modular feeder pathways converge at aminonitrile, \u03b1-KG, and hydantoin nodes, allowing for a diverse array of plausible geochemical scenarios. Pyroglutamate\u2014the cyclization product of glutamate\u2014is incorporated as a reversible reservoir node. All ten pathways ultimately deliver glutamate under non-enzymatic, prebiotic, iron-rich, anoxic conditions, in accord with geochemical and meteoritic constraints.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2=CH\u2013CHO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrolein\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O/c1-2-3-4/h2-3H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"OHCCH2CH2CN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-cyanopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"3-cyanopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H5NO/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h4H,1-2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H7N3/c6-3-1-2-4-5(7)8/h1-4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"glutamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glutamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H10N2O3/c6-3-1-2-4(8)5(9)7/h1-3H2,(H2,6,7)(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glutamic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(10)11)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,10,11)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxalic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h(H,3,4)(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydantoin-5-propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glutamate hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-oxopyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyroglutamic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H7NO3/c7-4-2-1-3-5(8)6-4/h1-3H2,(H,6,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"succinonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H6N2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h1-2H2,3-4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyanoacetylene\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyanoacetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3HN/c1-2-3-4/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Conjugate addition of HCN to acrolein\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"alkaline or near-neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic addition of HCN to the \u03b2 position of acrolein yields 3-cyanopropanal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is proposed based on the mechanism outlined in Van Trump & Miller (1972) and supported by Parker et al. (2011) as plausible for prebiotic settings; however, explicit glutamate yields remain undemonstrated for this sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of aminonitrile from 3-cyanopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"alkaline to neutral pH; classic Strecker aqueous conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amination and cyanide addition to the aldehyde to form the \u03b1-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Standard Strecker-type chemistry demonstrated robustly for other amino acids, plausible for this substrate (Johnson et al. 2008; Cleaves et al. 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of 2-aminopentanedinitrile to glutamine and/or glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"extended hydrolysis, wet-dry cycles beneficial\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis/subsequent amide formation to yield glutamine, which may hydrolyze further to glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Analogous nitrile hydrolysis is established in acid/base-catalyzed prebiotic aqueous chemistry (Patel et al. 2015). Complete hydrolysis is slow but plausible on wet-dry minerals.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glutamine to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mild base or mineral-catalyzed hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amide hydrolysis on mineral or in solution yields glutamate from glutamine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glutamine hydrolysis to glutamate is a key and chemically simple step, known to proceed in model systems (see cyanosulfidic schemes: Patel et al. 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreduction of cyanoacetylene to succinonitrile (cyanosulfidic photochemistry)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation (254 nm); HS- present\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV-assisted photoreduction of cyanoacetylene (with H2S) to the C4 dinitrile via copper-catalyzed redox photochemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated this sequence experimentally, including the step yielding succinonitrile as a C4 skeleton precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreduction of succinonitrile to 3-cyanopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"continued UV (254 nm)\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Selective reduction of one nitrile to an aldehyde, yielding 3-cyanopropanal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"An experimentally supported photoreduction in the Patel et al. (2015) cyanosulfidic network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate\u2013pyruvate condensation to hydroxyketoglutarate/oxopentenedioate and reduction to \u03b1-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe(II)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe(II) or phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral to mildly alkaline; can occur both with and without metal\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol addition followed by rearrangement and reduction yields \u03b1-ketoglutarate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Metal-catalyzed and metal-free \u03b1-KG formation from glyoxylate and pyruvate is robust (Muchowska et al. 2019; Stubbs et al. 2020).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoglutarate to glutamate (FeS, H2 or Ni/H2/HCO3)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"Ni (meteorite)\", \"H2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-350K (Ni/H2); 323-348K (FeS)\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS, Ni, or meteorite fragments\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"NH4+ or NH3 present; moderately alkaline\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive amination of the ketoacid yields glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nonenzymatic FeS- and Ni/H2-driven reductive amination established under hydrothermal and vent-analogue conditions (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003; Kitadai et al. 2019; Kaur et al. 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin formation from \u03b1-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293-313K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"DAP or NH3 as N-source; neutral to mildly basic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide and ammonia react with \u03b1-KG to form glutamate hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated prebiotic hydantoin formation on \u03b1-ketoacids with subsequent glutamate release; yields for hydantoin are high, but Glu is partial (Pulletikurti et al. 2022).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glutamate hydantoin to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"acidic or basic hydrolysis, wet-dry cycles increase rate\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ring opening of hydantoin and hydrolysis generates glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated experimentally with glutamate yield ~19% from hydantoin (Pulletikurti et al. 2022); high stability/reservoir for eventual release.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Nonenzymatic transamination: glycine + \u03b1-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"phosphate buffer or mineral surface; neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amino group transfer from glycine to \u03b1-KG yields glutamate; nonenzymatic exchange.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Metal-free transamination is robust for \u03b1-ketoacids (Stubbs et al. 2020); no catalyst required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of HCN oligomers yields glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"strong acid/base hydrolysis, requires history of HCN concentration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Random breaking of HCN oligomers releases various amino acids including glutamate upon hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) and previous studies establish this as a generic but very inefficient source of glutamate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization of glutamate to pyroglutamic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"promoted by low pH, wet-dry cycles, or thermally\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular cyclization of glutamate via the amino group to the gamma-carboxyl, forming a 5-membered ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Well-documented prebiotic side-reaction and reservoir; seen in meteorites and laboratory decompositions (Gayte-Sorbier et al.; Wu et al. 2010).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of pyroglutamate to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"acidic or basic hydrolysis, wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ring opening under hydrolysis conditions restores glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Observed in acid hydrolysis of meteorite extracts (Pizzarello & Cooper 2001). Pyroglutamate can thus act as a dynamic prebiotic reservoir of Glu.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal direct reduction of CO2 to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe-rich minerals\", \"H2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mixing zones, vent fluid\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Overall net reduction and amination of CO2 and NH3 to glutamate under reducing, Fe-rich hydrothermal vent conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported thermodynamically by Amend & Shock (1998), though strongly critiqued for lack of kinetic evidence (Bada & Miller 1995; Aubrey et al. 2009).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-acrolein Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN from electric discharge chemistry combines with acrolein to give 3-cyanopropanal, which undergoes Strecker synthesis to aminonitrile and then hydrolysis to glutamate via glutamine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic photochemical nitrile pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanoacetylene undergoes repeated photoreduction with H2S and UV, forming succinonitrile and then 3-cyanopropanal. Entry into the Strecker sequence leads to glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"\u03b1-Ketoglutarate metabolic analogue pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate and pyruvate condense (FeII/phosphate mineral catalysis) to \u03b1-ketoglutarate, then undergo reductive amination to glutamate under FeS or Ni/H2 conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin pathway on \u03b1-KG\",\n      \"description\": \"\u03b1-Ketoglutarate reacts with NH3 and HCN to form glutamate hydantoin, which hydrolyzes to glutamate. This provides a bridge between nitrile and metabolic routes.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Nonenzymatic glycine transamination pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate + pyruvate furnish \u03b1-KG, which then undergoes transamination with glycine to form glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN oligomer hydrolysis pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Concentrated HCN polymerizes under evaporitic/surface conditions; subsequent hydrolysis liberates glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyroglutamate hydroplay\",\n      \"description\": \"Any pathway that reaches free glutamate also enables cyclization to pyroglutamate and subsequent hydrolysis back, serving as a reservoir and sink for Glu under evaporative and hydrothermal fluctuations.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hydrothermal reduction pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Reduction and amination of CO2/NH3 in Fe-rich hydrothermal systems yield glutamate directly (theoretically favorable thermodynamics, debated kinetics).\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical C4 nitrile reservoir branch\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanoacetylene and H2S photoreduction produces succinonitrile and then 3-cyanopropanal, which can feed directly into the Bucherer\u2013Bergs condensation or aminonitrile formation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic/extraterrestrial delivery pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Glutamate, pyroglutamate, and their hydrolysis/condensation products can be delivered by meteorites, with hydrolysis releasing free glutamate in surface/evaporitic environments.\",\n      \"reaction_