_coherence\": 0.65,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.9,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.55,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.52,\n      \"feedback\": \"Cyclization to pyroglutamate (rxn_013) is generally plausible with water loss, but the back hydrolysis (rxn_014) is unbalanced and does not adequately specify the need for harsh or catalytic conditions (acidic pH, high temp). This pathway can reasonably serve as a reversible reservoir, but only when balances are explicit.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Balance water loss in cyclization and water/ammonia uptake in hydrolysis.\",\n        \"Cite conditions from Gayte-Sorbier et al. (pH, temperature).\",\n        \"Include rate constants if proposing reversibility.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.05\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.05,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.1,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.85,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.1,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.13,\n      \"feedback\": \"This single-step, direct hydrothermal CO2/NH3 \u2192 Glu route is not chemically valid as written: it lacks any plausible mechanism for the multicarbon and multistep transformations required, fails mass conservation, and is only supported in literature as a one-step thermodynamic analysis (Amend & Shock 1998) with strong kinetic and stability objections. Not experimentally validated.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Omit or replace with stepwise, literature-backed chemistry for C\u2013C and CN bond formation.\",\n        \"Only reference as a thermodynamic driver, not as a real pathway.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_016\": 0,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.2,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.2,\n  