1-2-6(3-4)5/h1-2H2,(H2,5,7)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C4H4N2O2", "iupac_name": "succinic semialdehyde", "common_name": "4-oxobutanoic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H6O3/c5-2-1-3-4(6)7/h2H,1,3H2,(H,6,7)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Beta-addition of HCN to acrolein forming 3-cyanopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["null"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "neutral to mildly basic pH"}, "mechanism": "Conjugate (Michael-type) addition of HCN to the \u03b2-carbon of acrolein yielding 3-cyanopropanal.", "reasoning": "Explicitly proposed as the C4 precursor step in Miller-type prebiotic chemistry; see Van Trump & Miller (1972), Parker et al. (2011). While never complete in a one-pot Glu synthesis, the chemistry is plausible and mass balanced under aqueous conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Strecker synthesis from 3-cyanopropanal to glutaminonitrile (\u03b1-aminonitrile)", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["null"], "conditions": {"temperature": "280-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "neutral to basic pH"}, "mechanism": "Sequential nucleophilic addition of ammonia and cyanide to the carbonyl of 3-cyanopropanal followed by proton transfers and formation of \u03b1-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Strecker chemistry is well established for small aldehydes and is plausible for this intermediate. Prebiotic conditions supported by Parker et al. (2011), Johnson et al. (2008), and analogous aldehyde Strecker."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Hydrolysis of glutaminonitrile to glutamic acid", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["null"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370K (evaporitic cycling)", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Wet-dry cycling with heating, repeated hydration"}, "mechanism": "Two-step hydrolysis of the aminonitrile: first to the amide, second to the amino acid, with stoichiometric consumption and production of water and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis is well supported in prebiotic chemistry; Miller experiments and HCN polymer hydrolysis demonstrate conversion to amino acids. Mass-balanced with water and ammonia byproducts (Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013; Parker et al. 2011)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cyanosulfidic photoreduction to 3-cyanopropanal from succinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_002", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II)", "H2S"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) (trace copper salt)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Strong UV (254 nm), presence of H2S as reductant"}, "mechanism": "UV-driven, Cu-photoredox cycle: two sequential photoreductions of succinonitrile in the presence of H2S yield the semialdehyde 3-cyanopropanal, liberating HCN and water.", "reasoning": "Experimentally reported by Patel et al. (2015, Nature Chemistry). Requires local sulfide, copper, and UV; per-step yields and balances inferred from full mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Polymerization of HCN to aminonitrile-rich HCN tetramers (DAMN-type intermediates)", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_002", "mol_002", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["null"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "High HCN concentration"}, "mechanism": "Stepwise condensation of four HCN molecules in mildly basic solution forms diamino-maleonitrile or related aminonitrile tetramers.", "reasoning": "Directly supported by Ferris et al. (1978), Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). Mass-balanced for 4 \u00d7 HCN \u2192 HCN tetramer; structure represents major prebiotic polymer intermediate to Glu via hydrolysis."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrolysis of HCN polymers to aminonitriles/amino acids (includes Glu fragment release)", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_014", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["null"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-360K; acid hydrolysis", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic pH, extended heating"}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of amidine-rich HCN polymers yields \u03b1-aminonitrile and pentanedioic fragments; water incorporated in hydrolytic cleavage.", "reasoning": "Reported in Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013); although structures in prebiotic mixtures are distributed, release of Glu-relevant aminonitrile units is supported. Water stoichiometry modular to polymer size; simplified here."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Aldol condensation of glyoxylate and pyruvate forming hydroxyketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fe2+"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-343K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly basic, phosphate buffer (if present)"}, "mechanism": "Fe2+- or phosphate-catalyzed aldol reaction: nucleophilic addition of glyoxylate to pyruvate forms hydroxyketoglutarate, which is dehydrated to oxopentenedioate and then reduced (in situ or by glyoxylate acting as reductant) to \u03b1-ketoglutarate, releasing CO2.", "reasoning": "Direct kinetics and yields from Muchowska et al. (2019, Nature), Stubbs et al. (2020). Reaction sequence mass-balanced by tracking CO2 loss from the reduction step."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoglutarate to glutamate", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_010", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["m