quivalents for full hydrolysis; mass balanced."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Transamination of \u03b1-ketoglutarate and glycine to glutamate and glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_008"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["null"], "conditions": {"temperature": "293-318K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "PLP-analogue possible, but not required"}, "mechanism": "Aminotransfer: Transfer of an amino group from glycine to the carbonyl carbon of \u03b1-ketoglutarate, yielding glutamate and glyoxylate.", "reasoning": "Nonenzymatic transamination under plausible prebiotic conditions demonstrated in Stubbs et al. (2020), Kaur et al. (2024). Mass and charge conserved."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Cyclization of glutamic acid to pyroglutamic acid (reversible dehydration)", "inputs": ["mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["null"], "conditions": {"temperature": ">333K (dry, heat)", "pressure": "ambient", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "pH 2\u20133.5 or dry heat"}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular attack of the amino group on the \u03b3-carboxyl yields a five-membered lactam (pyroglutamic acid), releasing water.", "reasoning": "Thermodynamically and kinetically supported by Wu et al. (2010), Weiss et al. (2018); this is a documented fate of prebiotic glutamate, especially under hydrothermal or evaporitic cycling. Mass-balanced."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Hydrolytic ring opening of pyroglutamic acid to glutamate", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["null"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-373K; wet environment", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic or neutral pH, extended heating, hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic/lactam ring opening with water to reform glutamic acid.", "reasoning": "Acid or water hydrolysis of pyroglutamate (as in m