n fragments) to balance each reaction.\",\n        \"Cite exact literature yields and conditions at each step.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.50,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.50\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.52,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.78,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.85,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.70,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.64,\n      \"feedback\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin pathway is experimentally well founded (Pulletikurti et al. 2022) but rxn_009 and rxn_010 are both flagged as unbalanced, with significant element mismatches. Downstream hydrolysis has additional imbalances from missing or unspecified byproducts. Mechanistic steps are realistic, but this pathway as drawn does not conserve mass/charge. It would benefit from a detailed annotation of all N/C/O flows and clear mapping of intermediates.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Show hydantoin ring opening/hydrolysis correctly, with all byproducts.\",\n        \"If noncanonical intermediates/species are formed, represent them explicitly.\",\n        \"Strengthen mechanistic justification for each transformation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.80,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.60\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.70,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.90,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.90,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.90,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.80,\n      \"feedback\": \"Transamination of \u03b1-KG with glycine is experimentally demonstrated in nonenzymatic, prebiotic analogues. rxn_011 (transamination) is chemically plausible and atom/charge conservation issues are mild or correctable. rxn_007 (preceding \u03b1-KG synthesis) bears the previously noted atom-mass gap, but does not directly impact the amination step's feasibility. This is one of the more plausible and literature-congruent pathways, with main risk being the \u03b1-KG supply step.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Clarify byproduct fate in the aldol condensation to maintain strict conservation.\",\n        \"Explicitly cite literature where glycine-mediated transamination conditions were used.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.60,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.60\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.60,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.80,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.85,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.80,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"The pyroglutamate reservoir cycle correctly models glutamate's known tendency to cyclize, with reversible ring opening/closure. Both rxn_012 and rxn_013 are flagged as unbalanced (minor O/H defects) but can likely be adjusted with small-molecule accounting. Mechanistically, cyclization/decyclization is well documented. This pathway isn't a means of net synthesis, but accurately represents crucial fate/sink reservoirs in prebiotic settings.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Show all byproducts (e.g., water loss/uptake or competing degradation products).\",\n        \"Reference experimental cyclization/ring-opening rates.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.50\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.50,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.60,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.70,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.70,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.63,\n      \"feedback\": \"The direct hydrothermal route is thermodynamically favorable at h