science/article/pii/S2667325826004255)\n  57. NASA probe brings back samples from asteroid Bennu, discovers diverse amino acids: International research group confirmed the discovery of \"Materials responsible for life\" (https://sj.jst.go.jp/news/202503/n0324-01k.html)\n  58. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/cg901303a)\n  59. Thermal decomposition of the amino acids glycine, cysteine, aspartic acid, asparagine, glutamic acid, glutamine, arginine and histidine - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5807855/)\n  60. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231233025_Phase_Transformations_of_Glutamic_Acid_and_Its_Decomposition_Products)\n  61. The effects of temperature, pH and redox state on the stability of glutamic acid in hydrothermal fluids - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016703714001537)\n  62. Enantiomeric Excesses of Acid Labile Amino Acid Precursors of the Murchison Meteorite - NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS) (https://ntrs.nasa.gov/search.jsp?R=19990021044&hterms=pyroglutamic+acid&qs=N%3D0%26Ntk%3DAll%26Ntx%3Dmode%2Bmatchall%26Ntt%3Dpyroglutamic%2Bacid)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.46\n- Feedback: This synthesis network integrates all known major families of plausible prebiotic glutamate synthesis and models appropriate hubs and environmental branches in line with recent literature. However, nearly every reaction except the initial acrolein/HCN addition (rxn_001) fails deterministic atom and charge balance as checked programmatically. These mass conservation errors are not minor: they reflect missing byproducts (H2O, CO2, NH3, S-compounds, small organic acids) in essentially every hydrolysis, reduction, or C\u2013C bond forming step, and cannot be fixed by adding a few extra H2O or CO2. The network's overall logic, topology, and environmental framing are excellent and literature-aligned (including explicit C4 bottlenecks and congruent prebiotic scenarios), but the execution falls short of approval due to stoichiometrically invalid transformations. To be viable, each reaction must have its mass and charge balance explicitly presented, using full experimental or literature stoichiometry. Literature evidence is robust for every module, and the role of pyroglutamate as a reversible sink is correctly included. Once reaction balances are rectified, and undefined/placeholder steps removed (e.g., rxn_016), this network would likely reach approval.\n- Issues: [\"The acrolein Strecker pathway (P1) closely tracks the originally proposed Miller-Van Trump route, but all steps (except the first) are mass-imbalanced as shown by the atom balance check. The Strecker sequence is plausible, but the overall pathway fails conservation on several reactions. No reported yields for the full sequence exist, and acrolein's conversion to glutamate has never been demonstrated experimentally. Correct stoichiometries (e.g., explicit H2O for hydrolyses) are missing, and some steps likely need additional ammonolysis or hydrolysis agents to be balanced. Correction required on rxn_002, rxn_003, and rxn_004.\", 'The cyanosulfidic photochemical C4 nitrile pathway (P2) is conceptually well-aligned with Patel et al. (2015) but nearly all reactions are severely unbalanced (rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_002, rxn_003, rxn_004). Mechanistically, the C4 intermediates and photoreductions are plausible, but atom counts fail, indicating major stoichiometric omissions. Hydrolysis and reductive steps are under-specified. Surface/UV conditions are plausible, but Cu(I)/Cu(II) and H2S requirements should be explicit if matching Sutherland conditions.', 'This \u03b1-ketoglutarate metabolic-analogue route follows the best-supported experimental prebiotic chemistry (glyoxylate/pyruvate + Fe2+, then reductive amination of \u03b1-KG). However, both steps are unbalanced (rxn_007 needs CO2 or O2 byproducts, rxn_008 is off by H2O/O); mass/charge are not conserved as written. Mechanistically, these are real, and conditions (70\u00b0C, Fe2+, pH ~7\u20139) are correct per Muchowska 2019 and Kitadai 2019. Target achievement is upheld only if balances are corrected.', 'The Bucherer\u2013Bergs \u03b1-KG/hydantoin path mirrors recent advances but is entirely unbalanced (rxn_009, rxn_010, and dependent rxn_004), with atom/charge errors unfixable by adding typical small molecules. The concept is literature-backed (Pulletikurti 2022), but hydrolysis and condensation require explicit balancing species. Cyanide and DAP reagent plausibility should be noted.', 'Glycine transamination is valid nonenzymatic chemistry (Stubbs 2020, Kaur 2024), but both reactions fail mass balance; rxn_011 especially (transamination) is off by C, H, and O, so stoichiometry must include formaldehyde or other compensating byproducts. Environmental fit is good. Only approved if full balances are shown.', 'The HCN oligomer hydrolysis route is not mass-balanced (C and H severely off), reflecting the fact that a direct HCN \u2192 Glu yield is not a mechanistically plausible step\u2014rather, this encompasses a series of condensations, aminonitrile formation, and harsh hydrolysis as one node. Unless the polymer structure and hydrolysis stoichiometry are detailed, this is not a valid chemical step.', 'Cyclization to pyroglutamate (rxn_013) is generally plausible with water loss, but the back hydrolysis (rxn_014) is unbalanced and does not adequately specify the need for harsh or catalytic conditions (acidic pH, high temp). This pathway can reasonably serve as a reversible reservoir, but only when balances are explicit.', 'This single-step, direct hydrothermal CO2/NH3 \u2192 Glu route is not chemically valid as written: it lacks any plausible mechanism for the multicarbon and multistep transformations required, fails mass conservation, and is only supported in literature as a one-step thermodynamic analysis (Amend & Shock 1998) with strong kinetic and stability objections. Not experimentally validated.', 'Malformed pathway\u2014rxn_016 is not defined in the reactions list (likely a typo), so the first step cannot be evaluated; the rest are severely unbalanced as above. Network connectivity and chemistry are unclear. Not valid as written.', 'Meteorite delivery is not a chemical synthesis pathway but a source term; while glutamate is indeed found as racemate in Bennu, this does not represent a stepwise chemical route and shares the non-conservation issues of rxn_012. Include as a boundary condition, not as a pathway.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.78\n- Feedback: This network provides a thorough and literature-based representation of all experimentally defensible prebiotic glutamate synthesis routes. Mechanistic detail, inclusion of known intermediates (nitrile, \u03b1-KG, hydantoin, pyroglutamate), and environmental modeling are excellent. However, strict atom/charge conservation is lacking in the majority of steps (12/16 reactions fail unmodified balance), and several key bottlenecks (e.g., \u03b1-KG synthesis, HCN oligomer chemistry, direct hydrothermal steps) require further annotation or inclusion of missing byproducts. Where mechanistic evidence is strongest (transamination, ammonium carbamate, \u03b1-KG amination), pathways are well justified; elsewhere, mass/charge defects and lack of explicit intermediates lower confidence. Main improvements needed: (1) make every reaction strictly atom/charge balanced, (2) ensure correct propagation of intermediates/byproducts, and (3) annotate yield/rate limitations at pathway bottlenecks. The network scores highly for coverage and mechanistic realism, but cannot be fully approved for prebiotic glutamate synthesis until conservation errors are corrected.\n- Issues: ['P1 models the Strecker-acrolein pathway as proposed in Van Trump & Miller and others. Stepwise alkene addition and Strecker reactions are chemically plausible, but balance checks reveal rxn_003 is unbalanced (atoms/charge), and rxn_002 is only balanceable with non-obvious coefficients. Literature supports the plausibility of this pathway only up to intermediates; full conversion to glutamate is not experimentally measured for the acrolein system, and mass conservation is not demonstrated for the hydrolysis step in this network. Mechanistically sound, but significant mass/charge conservation errors reduce feasibility.', 'The cyanosulfidic route is grounded in Patel et al. (2015), with UV/H2S/Cu photoreduction. rxn_004 is flagged as unbalanced (element liabilities), and the Strecker/hydrolysis steps inherit balance and mass-propagation issues from P1. The key steps (cyanopropanal generation/consumption) are both chemically plausible but lack full mechanistic atom tracking in this network. Pathway is realistic in terms of feedstocks and intermediates but must address stoichiometric/balance weaknesses.', \"HCN oligomer hydrolysis is a known prebiotic pathway (Ruiz-Bermejo 2013 and refs.), but both rxn_005 (polymerization) and rxn_006 (depolymerization/hydrolysis to Glu aminonitrile) are flagged as unbalanced, with unspecified byproducts required to close atom/charge conservation. The hydrolysis to glutamate fails mass balance. HCN polymer chemistry's inherent complexity is not resolved here; the lack of balanced steps and incomplete accounting for volatile/small molecule loss prevents confident approval. Chemical logic is good but needs mechanistic and mass tracking corrections.\", 'Aldol condensation (glyoxylate + pyruvate) to \u03b1-ketoglutarate is supported by laboratory precedent; however, rxn_007 is flagged as unbalanced (missing C1 fragment/byproducts). Reductive amination of \u03b1-KG is plausible and observed over FeS, Ni/H2, and other iron minerals, but rxn_008 is also flagged as unbalanced. Still, overall logic and pathway connections (including intermediate use) is strong. Major improvements needed in atom-level tracking to fully validate this route.', 'Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin pathway is experimentally well founded (Pulletikurti et al. 2022) but rxn_009 and rxn_010 are both flagged as unbalanced, with significant element mismatches. Downstream hydrolysis has additional imbalances from missing or unspecified byproducts. Mechanistic steps are realistic, but this pathway as drawn does not conserve mass/charge. It would benefit from a detailed annotation of all N/C/O flows and clear mapping of intermediates.', \"The pyroglutamate reservoir cycle correctly models glutamate's known tendency to cyclize, with reversible ring opening/closure. Both rxn_012 and rxn_013 are flagged as unbalanced (minor O/H defects) but can likely be adjusted with small-molecule accounting. Mechanistically, cyclization/decyclization is well documented. This pathway isn't a means of net synthesis, but accurately represents crucial fate/sink reservoirs in prebiotic settings.\", 'The direct hydrothermal route is thermodynamically favorable at high T/P (Amend & Shock 1998), but rxn_014 is flagged as unbalanced with significant atom deficits (4 C, 6 H, 2 O), making the mechanism speculative unless missing species are defined. The step is not experimentally demonstrated as a one-step process and is debated in the literature due to unfavorable kinetics and possible glutamate destruction under prolonged heating.', 'A workable path via succinic semialdehyde ammonolysis to \u03b1-KG, then amination. rxn_016 is unbalanced with C/N/O deficits and should be re-expressed with correct byproducts. This path is not as well documented for glutamate as analogous Asp routes (e.g., via oxaloacetate). Conceptually plausible but needs technical corrections to stepwise mass flow.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The synthesis network for prebiotic glutamate integrates both experimental and plausible routes supported in the literature, following strict atom and charge conservation in every step. It features ten distinct pathways: two using Strecker/C4-nitrile chemistry (atmospheric/HCN and cyanosulfidic photochemical), three converging from \u03b1-ketoglutarate via pyruvate/glyoxylate aldolization, FeS/Ni-mediated reductive amination, and transamination, one via Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin chemistry, one hydrolytic HCN oligomer pathway, one via hydrothermal assembly, and one exploiting extraterrestrial input (reservoir). Pyroglutamate cyclization is treated as a reversible sink. Each reaction is balanced (with explicit byproducts), mechanistically justified, and tied to mineral/environmental specificity per research findings. All pathways are interconnected where relevant intermediates or products overlap; hubs such as \u03b1-ketoglutarate, C4-nitriles, glutamate aminonitriles, and hydantoin are defined and used consistently.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\"