s. rxn_007 (reductive amination over FeS) is balanced and strongly supported (Muchowska et al., 2019; Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003). Environments/conditions are realistic and consistent. Fixing rxn_006 will resolve major chemical concerns.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Fix stoichiometry for rxn_006: account for all atoms, ensure glyoxylate formula is consistent.\",\n        \"List possible side-products (e.g., water, hydrogen) as in published mechanisms.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.60\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.57,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.81,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.92,\n      \"target_achievement\": 1.0,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.72,\n      \"feedback\": \"This Ni/H2 pathway mirrors Kaur et al. (2024), but rxn_006 (see P4) and rxn_008 (C5H6O5 + NH4+ + H2 -> C5H9NO4 + H2O) are both unbalanced (rxn_008: H+1, charge +1). Significant issues with stoichiometry need correction\u2014especially for charge balance (extra H+). Mechanistically, the route is highly feasible and conditions realistic. Correction would raise confidence substantially.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Adjust proton accounting in rxn_008 (produce H+ or realign charge sources).\",\n        \"Balance rxn_006 as noted in P4.\",\n        \"Check all intermediate atom flows for compatibility with literature yields.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.60\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.57,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.78,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.93,\n      \"target_achievement\": 1.0,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.71,\n      \"feedback\": \"The Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin pathway is well supported (Pulletikurti et al., 2022), but major atom counts are off for rxn_006, rxn_009 (C+1, H+1, N+1, O+2), and rxn_010 (imbalances for N and H). These need explicit balancing; otherwise, the path connects appropriately from \u03b1-KG through hydantoin to glutamate. Conditions are plausible. Side-products and reagent identities (DAP, water equivalents) should be specified as per experimental protocol.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Balance all steps explicitly (especially nitrogen and oxygen).\",\n        \"Expand on hydrolysis conditions (e.g., temperature, acid/base, water equivalents).\",\n        \"Reconcile \u03b1-KG sources/formulas across connected reactions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.55\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.55,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.77,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.92,\n      \"target_achievement\": 1.0,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.70,\n      \"feedback\": \"Nonenzymatic transamination is strongly supported (Stubbs et al. 2020), but rxn_006 remains unbalanced and rxn_011 (C-1, H-2) likewise has stoichiometric issues. Pathway design is appropriate, mechanism strong, but stepwise atom and charge conservation must be strictly enforced. Products/byproducts of transamination (CO2) should be clarified.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Balance all reactions in detail, list leaving and entering atom counts.\",\n        \"Account for CO2/byproducts in transamination per literature models.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.98,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.98\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 1.0,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 1.0,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.95,\n      \"target_achievement\": 1.0,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.98,\n      \"feedback\": \"The pyroglutamate reservoir/sink cycle accurately reflects the dominant fate of glutamate under both dry/wet and moderate/acidic heating, with reversible interconversion. Both reactions are balanced, mechanistically solid, and in strong agreement with experimental findings (Gayte-Sorbier et al.). Environmental context is properly considered (pH, oxygen). Nearly ideal.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"None required; maintain this level of detail in all reaction modules.\",\n        \"Quantify rates and equilibrium constants for various pH/T conditions as per literature.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.50\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.50,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.60,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.92,\n      \"target_achievement\": 1.0,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.58,\n      \"feedback\": \"The direct hydrothermal CO2 fixation to glutamate is defensible as a thermodynamic possibility (Amend & Shock 1998) but has major chemical issues: rxn_014 is highly unbalanced (C-4, H-5, O-3, charge +1). Experimentally, such a one-pot route is only feasible at high T/P and gives negligible yields, with decomposition dominant. Thus, feasibility is weak; use only as a hypothetical/thermodynamic constraint.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Balance rxn_014: either expand in steps (via TCA intermediates) or include all small-molecule byproducts.\",\n        \"Clarify environmental boundaries, e.g., upper temperature limits for Glu survival.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pat